summary: Duplicate directories, near-duplicates, and keeping a decision on a group across scans — deferred.

!! Duplicate directories

Two directories with identical contents are duplicates in the sense a user cares
about, and are not detected: the scan compares file bytes. A directory hash (a
Merkle fold over the children's `mfr_full_hash` and names) would fit the group
model; its invalidation is the hard part — every ancestor's hash dies with any
descendant's write.

!! Near-duplicates

Files that are ''nearly'' identical (a re-encoded video, a resized image) share
no hash and are out of scope. The similarity machinery scores file names, not
content, and the content-similarity hash (TLSH) is not implemented. Were it to
be, near-duplicates want another model than an exact group: a score between two
files, not an equivalence class.

!! Keeping a decision

A group is rebuilt by each scan and carries no user data. Once it does — "this
is the copy I keep" — the scan's find-or-create must preserve it across runs
(which the (size, hash) identity allows), and `mf duplicate clear` must stop
being harmless.
